  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">WASH and FSL Famine Response to the IDPs and Vulnerable Host Communities in the hard-to-reached areas of (Dilling, Lagawa and Al kurmuk) in South Kordofan and Blue Nile States</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtThe proposed project aims to address and respond to the famine needs of conflict-affected, displaced, and host communities in Dilling, Lagawa, and Al Kurmuk localities of South Kordofan and Blue Nile states. These localities have been gravely affected by the conflict between SAF and RSF, which led to massive displacement. This project will save lives and provide basic services to some of the most needy and vulnerable populations.ltbrgtThe proposed duration of the project is 12 months to reach 66,815 affected population (10,291 men, 25,278 women, 12,389 boys and 18,857 girls) including 2500 PWD by the provision of emergency famine response interventions, through establishing and rehabilitating community WASH infrastructures, access to sufficient clean water, continued optimal hygiene behavior, and improved sanitation facilities to mitigate disease transmission and address environmental health risks. This is in addition to enhancing the food security and livelihood of the targeted beneficiaries. ltbrgtWASH interventions will prioritize providing sustainable, clean drinking water sources to 57,500. This will be done by drilling 4 new boreholes, rehabilitating 17&#xA0;existing hand pumps, rehabilitating 5 MWY/ WY with the extension of networks to support the FSL, water quality testing, and chlorination of 6&#xA0;Water Yards.ltbrgtThe project will also support the promotion of hygiene and sanitation by provision of Hygiene and Sanitation services to 17,000 IDPs and host communities, through training of hygiene promoters for 17 groups, Conduct of 34 hygiene promotion awareness, Construction of 3&#xA0;communal VIP latrines, Distribution of 250 hygiene kits and distribution of 8,000 bars of soap to the health centers.ltbrgtThis project will also provide lifesaving&#xA0;and emergency food security and livelihood inputs and services to 9,360 IDPs and vulnerable host communities in Dilling locality. The activities include the provision of improved winter season vegetable and legume seeds to 1000 HHs, training of 100 farmers in good agricultural practices, treatment of 10,000 head of livestock, training of 10 community animal health workers, and restocking of goats to 100 HHs.ltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
